function profileDialog(){showModal('Compiler profile & compatibility',`<div class="notice"><b>SharpForge 0.14 · managed/browser profile.</b> Real PE/CLI output, two managed execution engines, 44 dockable tools and 25 reusable packages. Not full C#, CLR, Windows App SDK or Visual Studio equivalence.</div><h3>C# and frequently used BCL</h3><p>Classes, partial declarations, overloads, properties, arrays, switch, checked arithmetic, deterministic cleanup, supported delegates/events and cooperative async tasks. New regular/verbatim interpolation supports alignment and selected invariant formats. Closed List, HashSet, Queue and Stack types accept int, double, bool, string and object; Dictionary keys are int/string. Collection initializers, indexers and versioned foreach enumerators execute in the managed heap. StringBuilder, common String helpers and additional Math functions are included. The supported framework API inventory describes exact overloads; general user generics, LINQ, full culture/comparer semantics and the complete BCL remain outside the profile.</p><h3>Language 14 and preview 15</h3><p>Supported field-backed properties and null-conditional assignments, target-typed construction, collection expressions/spreads, and explicitly selected preview capacity arguments and labeled loops. These feature gates do not imply complete conformance. LangVersion comes from project XML, or the Language &amp; Runtime tool for loose source.</p><h3>Numerics and networking</h3><p>Real WebAssembly SIMD128 kernels with scalar fallback; isolated numerical worker jobs with owned buffers. Array, seeded Random and bounded JSON contracts extend the BCL. HttpClient performs explicit-origin requests with cancellation, response limits and no ambient cookies or redirects. The separate JavaScript package supports bounded HTTP and WebSocket clients. CORS/CSP still apply. No raw sockets, shared-heap C# OS threads or native ClientWebSocket compatibility is implied.</p><h3>Code and design</h3><p>The WinUI designer links to declarative Create, InitializeComponent or Main construction methods without executing source. Minimal property edits preserve surrounding code. Structural edits require a proven construction region; handlers elsewhere remain unchanged. Dynamic expressions and unsupported statements stay code-owned. Candidate compilation and version checks protect the running compiler and reject conflicting updates. Design, Split, C# and Preview modes share the original source editor.</p><h3>WinUI web runtime</h3><p>HTML controls and layout, independent templates, local/template/style/default values, supported drawing surfaces, wrapping panels and RenderTransform. DoubleAnimation/Storyboard playback supports easing, repeat, AutoReverse, seeking, fill behavior and managed Completed callbacks. Clocks freeze at debugger stops. Canvas2D/DOM fallbacks are available; this is not native XAML, WinRT or Composition/VisualStateManager parity.</p><h3>Symbols and managed debugging</h3><p>Portable PDB reading/writing, verified source, ordinary-DLL source/instruction debugging, conditional/function/write breakpoints, run-to-cursor, safe watches, explicit effectful managed evaluation and guarded Set Next Statement. Threads/Parallel Stacks describe cooperative contexts, not OS threads. Hot Reload preserves compatible managed state; structural additions and local mapping are supported in the source VM, while direct-CIL updates require stable metadata and layouts. Reverse history restores bounded managed snapshots, not external effects.</p><h3>Workspace and toolchains</h3><p>Project/solution wizards, csproj/slnx and structural sln loading, source-combined project references, ZIP/folder round trips and conflict-checked native disk actions. The separately installed native host invokes the user's actual SDK/MSBuild engine only after explicit trust. Browser compilation does not silently emulate native tasks or arbitrary NuGet dependencies.</p><h3>Boundaries</h3><p>No general class inheritance, user-defined generic types, full numeric frontend, nullable analysis, general patterns or native CLR attachment. Accepted syntax is not evidence of complete language conformance. Roslyn extension DLLs require native builds; browser generators/analyzers are trusted JavaScript. The non-moving collector and interpreters are not a generational GC or JIT. Source edits reparse and rebind the compilation; editor highlighting uses a viewport but retains a whole-document text buffer. Native SDK, physical WebGPU and external IDE interoperability require separate qualification.</p>`,{wide:true});}
function architectureDialog(){const modules=[["text", "UTF-16 snapshots, spans and line maps"], ["syntax", "Tokens, parser, trivia and interpolated expressions"], ["compiler", "Binding, diagnostics and typed managed code lowering"], ["bytecode", "Predecoded instructions and verification"], ["cil", "PE/CLI metadata, MSIL emission, inspection and rebuilding"], ["runtime", "Managed heap, both interpreters, tasks, BCL and animation bridges"], ["debugger", "Breakpoints, stepping, reverse state, evaluation and updates"], ["workspace", "Versioned documents and compilation orchestration"], ["language", "Symbols, completion, navigation and language services"], ["protocol", "LSP/DAP adapters and installed stdio hosts"], ["editor", "Viewport highlighting, buffers, keyboard modes and editing"], ["refactoring", "Compile-checked source transformations"], ["extensions", "Trusted generators, analyzers and additional files"], ["project-system", "Projects, solutions, membership and workspace I/O"], ["docking", "Split groups, tabs, floating, auto-hide and popouts"], ["msbuild", "Explicit-trust native build and filesystem host"], ["controls", "Virtualized tree, nested menus and commands"], ["symbols", "Portable PDBs and verified source binding"], ["framework", "Closed API contracts, metadata and shared animation clock"], ["winui", "HTML controls, style/template engine and rendering fallbacks"], ["archive", "Bounded ZIP reading and deterministic export"], ["templates", "Project, solution and item plans"], ["designer", "Validated design model, code generation and C# synchronization"], ["compute", "Real WebAssembly SIMD kernels and isolated workers"], ["network", "Explicit-grant HTTP and WebSocket client transports"]];showModal('Modular architecture',`<div class="notice"><b>25 reusable packages.</b> Source and registry contracts are shared; Studio composes them instead of duplicating compiler or control logic.</div><pre>C# source ↔ syntax/spans ↔ design document
     ↓                         ↓
Binder → IR → PE/CLI + PDB     WinUI preview
                 ↓             ↕
        source VM / CIL VM ↔ bounded UI commands
                 ↕
    managed debugger / LSP / DAP</pre><div class="module-grid">${modules.map(([name,desc])=>`<div class="module-card"><b>@sharpforge/${name}</b><small>${desc}</small></div>`).join('')}</div><h3>Worker boundaries</h3><p>A compiler worker owns versioned source. A separate runtime worker owns managed execution. Designer candidate validation does not replace the active compilation; source versions and design revisions are checked again before applying text edits. The main thread renders HTML controls and drawing surfaces, but managed handlers execute in the VM. Storyboard clocks advance independently between managed callbacks and stop at debugger pauses.</p><h3>Managed state</h3><p>The tracing heap includes objects, arrays, BCL collection state and parked task frames. Both engines use generation-checked handles and bounded execution. Portable PDBs bind verified external source; the SharpForge stream additionally describes the canonical browser profile. Optional reverse history, explicit function evaluation and compatible code updates preserve or restore managed state, not external side effects.</p><h3>Distribution</h3><p>The normal static build uses local modules and self-contained workers. The standalone HTML bundles the same implementation, inline styles and Blob workers. Native SDK builds are a separate opt-in loopback process with OS permissions, not a browser sandbox. Read the supported API inventory and release validation for compatibility and qualification boundaries.</p>`,{wide:true});}
function aboutDialog(){showModal('SharpForge Studio',`<div class="notice"><b>C# in. JavaScript underneath.</b><br>A modular compiler, managed browser runtime and source-level debugging workbench.</div><p>Version 0.14.0 · MIT licensed implementation<br>Original Visual Studio-inspired interface. Not affiliated with Microsoft.</p><h3>Start here</h3><p>Build with Ctrl+Shift+B. Press F5 to run to a breakpoint. From idle, F10 or F11 explicitly stops at the first source statement. Use F10 to step over, F11 to step into a call, and Shift+F11 to step out. Click a gutter line to set a breakpoint; right-click it to add a condition or a logpoint.</p><p>Your workspace is recovered from browser-local storage. Export a project for a durable copy. Managed code executes locally; explicitly granted networking can send data to the allowed origins. Explicit native host workflows send selected files and build requests to your loopback host.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap"><button class="button" id="about-architecture">Architecture</button><button class="button" id="about-profile">Language profile</button><button class="button" id="about-shortcuts">Shortcuts</button></div>`);$('#about-architecture').onclick=architectureDialog;$('#about-profile').onclick=profileDialog;$('#about-shortcuts').onclick=shortcutsDialog;}
